It seems that every person I know at DEC decided to leave a day (or 
four :-) early for Labor Day.

We currently have 18 DECstation 3100's.  10 of them are at one site,
and the remainder at a second.  Recently, the ten system site has been 
seeing a serious amount of "Sorry, PID <> was killed on swap error" 
messages.  It began with one machine (admittedly one that seems to be
a bit more sensitive than the rest), but I'm now running with at least
60% of the machines reporting errors.  Each machine has an internal
RZ24.

The machines at the other site have not recorded even a single swap
error (though they do show a few more NFS timeouts, which because of
the configuration doesn't surprise me...)

This makes it seem like environment is the culprit.  Most of these
errors occur during the night, but a noticeable number occur during
the day when the rooms are actually reasonably cool and not particularly
humid.

If anyone has any theories of the cause or culprit, I'd appreciate 
hearing it.  These swap errors, besides being annoying, do occasionally
manage to lock the individual machine into an unstable state, which
when I force a reboot, annoys the heck out of people who run jobs that
take hours or days to complete.

Thoughts?  Incantations?

