Environment may very well be your culprit....but don't limit your thoughts to
only ambient air temp/humidity.   Take a look at power.  If need be, get a
dranitz (sp?) line monitor that can continously monitor power conditions.

I have on more than one occasion (with many, many different vendor's equipment
also) heard stories of things like janitor's plugging industrial vacum cleaners
into the powerstrip or circuit that the computer(s) happens to be plugged into. 

This kind of activity can cause the problems you are seeing.  Other possible
cuplrits could be further down the line (i.e. your power company), but you'll
have to find out yourself.

Everytime I hear "it only happens overnight" I get suspicious.

One more thing to check along w/ air temp & humidity is possible static causes.


