BACKGROUND: DIGITAL EQUIPMENT AND
MASSIVELY PARALLEL COMPUTING
 August 1991


                SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT KEY TO GROWTH

             FOR MASSIVELY PARALLEL PROCESSING MARKET

     The market for computers based on massively parallel 
processing (MPP) promises to grow substantially in the 1990s. 
Expectations vary, but some industry observers project market
growth to as much as $1 billion by 1995.
     Because application development software is critical to 
achieving this growth, Digital Equipment is focusing its resources 
on strategic development technologies and activities:

     o    Developing and enhancing application development tools --
          Digital will extend development tool components of its
          CASE (Computer Aided Software Engineering) product family
          to work with massively parallel architectures, and will
          pursue advance development in other key technologies such
          as language compilers, data libraries and networking
          technologies;

     o    Stimulating third-party development activities -- Digital
          will pursue partnerships and other initiatives to assist
          third-party application developers with creation of
          applications that can take advantage of massively
          parallel architectures.

     Digital expects these efforts to help encourage user and
developer participation and to thus open newer application areas
in ways that will fuel market growth across major industry
sectors in the 1990s.



                       ADDING DIGITAL VALUE
     Basic application development tools such as Digital's Language
Sensitive Editor (LSE), the Friendly UNIX Software Environment
(FUSE), and Digital Test Manager regression-testing facilities will
be extended to work with massively parallel architectures.  These
will help developers gain significant benefits of automation and
speed in designing, coding and testing complex massively parallel 
applications.  
     Also, Digital will work to parallelize existing libraries of
data objects in disciplines such as math and image processing, and
will investigate requirements and opportunities for future
parallelized data libraries.
     Digital is currently engaged in advanced research and
development on newer parallel compiler techniques, as well as on
ultra high speed parallel networking technologies.  These will be
applied to massively parallel architectures as they evolve, and as
the architectures mature.
     To encourage third-party development efforts, Digital is
committed to providing both technical assistance and hardware and 
software resources independent software developers.          

                 RESEARCH INTO PARALLEL PROCESSING
     For years Digital has supported partnerships with universities
and independent researchers in parallel processing research.

     One such research program, the Data Parallel Research
Initiative (DPRI), was established two years ago by Digital to
stimulate development of applications and operating system software
for massively parallel computer architectures.
     Another Digital sponsored effort with smaller scale parallel
processing is the Topaz Program, a research project involving
multiprocessor workstations and custom multiprocessing operating
system software.  Topaz, which benefits from multi-university
collaboration in the US, the UK, the Netherlands and Canada, is one
of many Digital sponsored university research projects currently
underway.
     Recent Digital internal MPP research has yielded several
patents for Digital, in data-routing and other technologies. 
Earlier Digital research into parallel processing with older style
Digital PDP-11 and 11/780 minicomputers brought about subsequent
advances in commercial computing technologies such as symmetric 
multiprocessing and clustering, where Digital today is the 
acknowledged technology leader with its VAXcluster systems.
          Commercial, business critical applications now feature
both clustering and SMP computing styles for customers in a wide
range of industries.


               HOW MASSIVELY PARALLEL COMPUTERS WORK
     Massively parallel processing is an outgrowth of the quest to
speed computer processing.  The traditional computer architecture
(known as the von Neumann architecture) operates in serial fashion. 
With every cycle of a central computer clock, the central processor
(CPU) processes a fixed-size instruction, or word.  The larger the
word length, from 16 to 32 bits, for instance, and the faster
the processor, the greater the volume of information that can be
processed in a set time.
     However, the faster the CPU, the more memory and larger
input/output bandwidth which is required to maintain balanced, high 
speed operations.  Multiple-processor implementations, with four, 
six, or even a hundred or more processors, can bring significant
increases in speed, but they must be coordinated by additional
logic in order to maintain synchronization, and this interprocessor
overhead can add considerably to internal resource utilization.
     Massively parallel architectures employ thousands of
relatively inexpensive processor/memory units, distributed
throughout the system.  MPP architectures are thus very efficient
at handling large data arrays (of 1,000 by 1,000 elements, for
instance), where the instruction flow can be parallelized to
operate on all elements simultaneously.
     As a result, massively parallel computers are considered to
have the potential, as more application software becomes
available, for handling a broadening range of highly complex
problem-solving applications.

                       CURRENT APPLICATIONS
     The newer massively parallel architectures make it possible to
process computationally intensive applications with ultra high
speed and relatively low cost.  For example, a 16,000-processor
machine is rated at 26,000 peak MIPS and 1,200 peak MFLOPS, and
costs just over $1 million.
     Some typical applications are:

     o  Structural analysis -- used frequently in automotive and
        aerospace industries, this relates to finite element 
        analysis of mechanical structures, both at rest and under
     	stress;
        
     o  Computational fluid dynamics -- the fastest growing area of 
        computational physics, this typically involves the study of 
        aerodynamics, including airflow over airfoils and air 
        resistance of automobiles;

     o  Seismic/reservoir modeling -- these are the two most used 
        oil-industry applications;

     o  Signal and image processing -- used extensively by NASA and 
        the military, this application requires very fast filtering
     	and analysis of electronic signals;

     o  Chemistry/biology -- these applications include studies of 
        chemical structures, reactions and properties, as well as 
        genetic molecular modeling;

     o  Commercial -- the major potential today for business/office 
        users involves database, financial-modeling and text-
     	retrieval applications.

     As software development tools become available, these and 
other application areas stand to benefit from the cost-effective 
dynamics of massively parallel architectures.  Newer products and 
cross-platform standards initiated by Digital and other major 
systems vendors will contribute to more profound market synergies, 
and massively parallel computers will become increasingly 
commonplace in mainstream computing installations.
