In article <JTKOHL.91Aug20165028@wrl.pa.dec.com>, jtkohl@pa.dec.com (John T. Kohl) writes:
> It seems to me upon investigation that perhaps the Scholar Plus will
> only talk MNP to a similar Scholar Plus modem, and not to some other
> manufacturer's modem.
> 
> Can anybody prove that last sentence false?  Please?

  Unfortunately, you're right. The MNP in the S+ doesn't conform to "real"
MNP. This is odd, since when you buy a modem chipset and program license,
one of the things you get is a working MNP implementation. I know DEC wan-
ted to add the DMCL command set, but it seems that the MNP got broken some-
where along the way.

  I read an article in Digital [Something] about a year ago where someone
in DEC's modem group was asked why DEC made modems. The answer was that they
were for customers who wanted to buy a complete solution from DEC. As far as
I can see, that's the only reason. I've fought with various DEC modems from
the DF03 on, and they have _all_ had some stupid defect. The DF124 wouldn't
do 1200 _and_ 2400 - it had to be set one way or the other. The 224 tended
to drop dead and had some odd EIA handshaking problem. The 242's MNP doesn't
work right. The 196/296 originally wouldn't talk to other V.32 modems, and
also had MNP problems. They seem to have fixed that, but a larger than normal
number of DF196/296's seem to forget they're alive - they just stop working
until the power is cycled. Besides this, they're all overpriced and under-
featured compared to the rest of the industry. I wish they would learn a les-
son from some other product groups and just buy somebody else's modems and
slap DEC bumper stickers on them. _ANYBODY_ else's modems.

  Having said all that, you [John, that is] might want to talk to Bob Early
in the modem group about the MNP problem. He seems to be quite helpful, al-
though changing an older product isn't possible, he may be able to give you
some S-register settings to make the handshake timing more tolerant. Then
you could report back to us...

