Well, I've received a couple of messages and decided to go with some DEC
VT420s (we get a nice price break here).  Here are the messages and my
comments.

__________
From: Happy Hacker <gary_p@vcs.com>
Subject: Terminals

A while ago we ran a pretty big terminal place (60 terms)

We tried a lot of terminals.  Here's my opinions of them:

Espirit Opus terminals:  TOTAL TRASH.  We got in 10 of these.  They a 1 yr
warrenty which we got them to extend to two years after their poor
performance.  We had over 20 repairs on these 10 terminals with most repairs
being entire unit swaps over U.P.S. shipping.  This meant downtime and
terminal loss.  We never could get them working.  They were BAD.  First it
was the power supplies but when we had all 10 swapped as they lost power,
the video started going.  When we had all 10 swapped again (over months of
failures) we then started losing setup every time they were powered off but
we lived with that error since the cost ($10 to $20) to ship them back and
get another defective unit wasn't worth it. AVOID THESE.  Tell your friends!

Wyse 85A: Standard vt220 clone.  Good solid terminal, good price, high
reliability (far surpassing DEC VT220 reliability), good features.  We
bought 20 of them. [This is surprising - we have a bunch of Wyse 30s, and I
haven't been impressed by their reliability -pw]

HP 220 clone: An OK terminal.  Some _hated_ the keyboard, some loved it.  It
was a love/hate terminal.  Our only sample died 6 months out due to a
keyboard failure (internal membrane wire broke).  Cost to fix wasn't worth
it.  We never tried enough to get a good feel on HP.

DEC VT220: We had a bunch of these.  Average failure rate of maybe 2 or 3 a
year out of 20.  Wyse is much better.  No terminal seemed to be user
serviceable so a failure means $200 usually.  A real bummer.  DEC was the
biggest problem because they fail almost often enough to make a service
contract worth it.  Wyse's just don't fail enough and esprits fail too much. 
DEC's are an adequate terminal.  We hated the 320's character set especially
the M.  A 420 looks pretty hot.  We just got one it.  Dual sessions (2 rs232
wires) and pretty cool.

Decide if you LOVE MMJ (mangled modified jacks) from DEC before you buy dec
terminals and servers.  At one employer we avoided DEC because of this but
here we just ran all MMJ stuff (buy your mmj parts from Altex in texas!
CHEAP!  Please post this.  MMJ parts: Altex in texas). [The department has
lots of DEC stuff, so this is no problem for us -pw]

Gary
vcs!gary_p@ucdavis.edu

----------
From: jerry@marin.nbn.com (Jerry C. Hudgins)
Subject: Re: comments on text terminals?

I've been very impressed with the VT420, and I've been using DEC terminals
(and their emulators) since the VT50.  The screen quality is excellent, the
thing supports speeds up to 38.4 KBaud, it supports two sessions (either w/
SSU or two lines), and it has that neat local cut and paste feature.  I like
it a great deal, even better than my VT330.  Price is right, too.  While
they're too new for any long-term reliability experience, I haven't seen any
problems with them yet.

---------- 
One last comment - we have quite a few Zenith Z29s which are old, but seem
to be holding up pretty well.

That's all folks.

Paul


