For those interested in VAXELN, the following is an article I submitted
to a local LUG a couple of years ago.

     
                        A Real Time Alternative - VAXELN
     
                              by Craig L. Sturgeon
     
     
     In the past Dofasco was selecting RSX solutions for real time 
     applications.  RSX is an event driven operating system allowing the 
     developers to have control and the synchronization of their software.  
     We lived with RSX's headaches, including, 64 Kbyte limitations, 
     overlaying, windowing, flat directory structure, and file name 
     limitations.  As RSX matured we saw improvements in memory management, 
     performance, software tools, commands, and I/D space - how did we ever 
     survive without it?
     
     Software developers using VMS could laugh at us, with their virtual 
     memory, DCL command language, software development tools, and tree 
     directory structures.  We were envious, however, we could respond to 
     real events, could they?  VMS gave them the a modified round-robin 
     scheduler.  Each process in the VMS system was given a time slice 
     until either: a higher priority process became computable; the process 
     is no longer computable (because of a resource wait); the process 
     itself voluntarily entered a wait state; or the time slice ended.  
     There was an exception to this rule.  VMS supports "real time" 
     processes, processes with a priority greater than 15.  "Real time" 
     processes followed the above rule except they are not restricted to 
     time slicing.  For some real time applications VMS does the trick, 
     however if you have a critical, high speed application, VMS's overhead 
     may not perform.
     
     An alternative to look at is VAXELN.  VAXELN has been described as 
     "lean and mean".  Lean, because it does not have as much of the 
     overhead as VMS.  Mean, for the same reason.  A VAXELN system image is 
     entirely in memory, therefore, their is no paging.  VAXELN is a 
     multi-user system having its own command language, ECL.  ECL is a 
     subset of VMS's DCL.  There are no development tools.  You cannot edit 
     a file with LSE or EDT - directly that is, you could do it from a VMS 
     system across the network.  VAXELN does have a tree directory 
     structure.
     
     VAXELN is a VMS layered product.  It comes as a tool kit.  Therefore 
     ELN must be developed on a VMS host system, allowing ELN developers 
     to use any or all the VMS development tools, including LSE, MMS, CMS, 
     etc.  The kit includes all the libraries to generate FORTRAN, C, 
     PASCAL, and ADA program images.  The kit also comes with a few ELN 
     tools, and a few VMS development tools.  When you complete your 
     application, developers use a tool called EBUILD, to generate a VAXELN 
     system image.  You then use EDEBUG, or NCP to trigger the target node 
     with your system image.
     
     VAXELN is what I've labeled as a "true" multi-tasking system.  VAXELN 
     allows you to write a program with several simultaneously executing 
     parts.  In other words, I can write a program that can get subroutines 
     to execute simultaneously with the calling module.  A program that is 
     loaded and running in a VAXELN system is called a job.  Each 
     simultaneously execute part in a job is called a process.  
     
     VAXELN is an event driven operating system.  A process executes until 
     there is a significant event.  A significant event is one of the 
     following: a higher-priority process within the selected job enters 
     the Ready state; a process in a higher-priority job enters the Ready 
     state; a job or process changes priority; the processor's internal 
     millisecond clock or another device issues a hardware interrupt.
     
     VAXELN is truly a "lean" operating system and it is a just as "mean".  
     For example, error exception handling must be handled by the 
     application developer.  Process and job priorities are reverse from 
     that of VMS - zero is the highest priority.  Priorities are actually 
     reversed in VMS so its easier for users to understand.  For VAXELN, 
     they decided VAXELN developers do not require the few instructions 
     added to the system to reverse the prority levels.
     
     VAXELN is not yet mature, however, it is maturing.  ECL becomes more 
     useful with each release as does debugging facilities.  Perhaps in 
     future releases, DEC can keep VAXELN "lean", and remove some of the 
     "mean".
     
