If the DECstation 5000/200 is like the 5000/120 in this respect, and I believe
it is, then no, you can't hook up a BW monitor in addition to a color monitor.
In addition to the normal 1-monitor configuration, Ultrix 4.2 allows the
following two configurations:

	- two black and white monitors
	- two unaccelerated color monitors.

Also, I believe you need a video card for each monitor.

I happen to have a black and white and a color monitor, and they can't be
used together.  I believe I heard some excuses from DEC about how X doesn't
work very well with two screens of different color depths, but it's clearly
not an insurmountable problem: I have a macintosh with a color and a black
and white monitor, and I can use X on it, with color and/or black and
white windows on the color and/or black and white monitors.  E.g. I can open
a color window so that half of it is on the color monitor and half on the
black and white monitor.  Incidentally, my macintosh has had a color and a 
black and white monitor since 1988.  It seems to me that DEC engineering
is second-rate in this respect.

Is it just my imagination or is DEC engineering second-rate in other respects
also?  For example, you can't mix memory modules of different sizes, which is
possible on a macintosh.

My final complaint (while I'm at it) is that despite DEC's talk about
adhering to standards and being open, they are far from it.  You can't use standard memory modules.  You can't use DEC SCSI devices on other computers. 

Oh, one more complaint.  Ultrix 4.2 came with a 2-user license.  The way this
is interpreted, if I log in at the console and I log in from the macintosh in 
my office, then I can't log from anywhere else (e.g. home).  What this means
in practice is that even though there is only one user involved, the "2-user
license" is a real nuisance, forcing me to log out and back in all the time.

I saved money by buying a DECstation (DEC has a 60% discount program with
the university of Pittsburgh) but I'm starting to regret it...

Harry Plantinga
University of Pittsburgh
planting@cs.pitt.edu


