> The general problem of windows crossing onto multiple screens is more or less
> unsurmountable, unless you wish to reduce things to a least common denominator.
> Think of the problems of a 32 bit 3D display and a single bit monochrome display
> sometime.  The MAC doesn't face as wide a diversity of display types, or attempt
> to provide some of the facilities X does.

The way that apple handles this problem is that all applications write to quickdraw and quickdraw renders appropriately according to the graphics hardware in use.  In fact, you can have a window that is partially on an unaccelerated
black and white display and partially on an accelerated 24-bit color display.

It is true that this arrangment makes it difficult or impossible to use a 
true 3D accelerator.  The only accelerators possible are ones that accelerate
quickdraw commands, and quickdraw is 2D.  (In fact, I suppose that this is the very reason I bought a DECstation!)

> > You can't use DEC SCSI devices on other computers. 
> 
> Hmm... I've certainly heard of people using our disks on other machines, and other
> people's disks on our machines.

I've successfully used a Maxtor disk on this system.  However, I tried
to use the 209-MB internal disk on another system, but the disk doesn't 
support the SCSI read geometry command, whereas the original connor 
version, on which it is based, does.  If DEC had not put in a different 
ROM or had made the drive respond to standard SCSI commands, I could have 
used it.

The other problem I had was in trying to use a TZK10 tape drive on another
system.  The software I have didn't know about DEC TZK10 tape drives, so
they wouldn't work together.  I don't know if better software would have
worked, but again, if DEC had not changed the ROMS in the tape drive, it
would have worked for me.

Perhaps there is a good reason for changing the ROMS that I do not know about?

