In one of the Berkeley RAID articles which reviewed high performance disk
architectures, there is a reference to a DEC publication entitled 
"Digital Large Systems Mass Storage Handbook".  It appears to cover,
among other things, DEC's distributed storage architecture as used 
in VAXclusters.  I tried ordering it from DEC, but they couldn't find 
it in their list.  Two different people who work on DEC storage products 
at Colorado Springs told me that it is given to customers attending their 
Storage Days symposia, but that these customers were all under non-disclosure.
One guy checked into it and found that this proprietary handbook has been 
cleansed and is now available with a Digital part number.  I got a copy 
when I was in Colorado Springs recently.

This is a great handbook!  It covers a lot of background on the various 
types of storage, everything from the difference between start/stop and 
streaming tape drives through designs for memory caches.  In particular,
it has sections describing DEC's distributed storage architecture, 
including MSCP (Mass Storage Control Protocol).  The actual MSCP 
specification is Digital proprietary, but the handbook lays out the 
general ideas.  Here are the chapters in the handbook:

1.  Introduction
2.  Memory Technology
3.  The Digital Storage Architecture
4.  The Digital Storage Interconnects
5.  Magnetic Disk Technology
6.  Magnetic Tape Technology
7.  Optical Technology
8.  Controllers
9.  Database Management
10. Storage Subsystem Performance

The guy who gave me a copy said he only had a limited number.
I figured it couldn't cost too much, so I called DEC for the list
price.  It turns out that this handbook is under "promotional literature"
and is free for the asking.  The lady I talked to had 1800 copies on
hand and was ready to send me as many copies as I needed.

To receive your free copy of this great handbook:

call DEC promotional literature at (508) 351-4401
ask for the "Digital Storage Technology Handbook"
part number EC-H0374-45/90A


-- 
