|>Our DECstation 5000/200CX is running Ultrix 4.1 and Fortran for RISC.

You might think about upgrading to the current products (ULTRIX V4.2 and
DEC FORTRAN V3.0.). However...

|>(1)  When I compile a Fortran program with a large array A(2*N,N), say N=400,
|>the compiled program give segament fault (core dumped).  The program runs
|>OK for smaller N.

Compile your program with '-static' or use 'limit stacksize unlimited'.
The default for FfR is dynamic, stack-based local storage. If you can't fit
your locals on the stack, it'll seg fault. I know, it's a pretty stupid
way to tell you you've exceeded your stack limit.

|>(2) I also tried to convert a pascal program to c using ptoc (come with Ultrix
|>software distribution as unsupported subset).  The pascal program compiles
|>and runs OK on a VAX, but the ptoc gives "segament fault (core dumped)"
|>too.  The source code actually is not long at all, just 120 lines.

No idea here.

|>(3)  I installed dvitovdu for tex/latex, but it seems always ask some more
|>*pxl fonts.  How I can get them all?  Convert all *pk to *pxl?
|>I cannot get xdvi compiled, due to incomplete X11-library from DEC.  Any
|>compiled version exist?  GNUPlot's X11 make also fails due to the same
|>reason.   Any ideas?

Install ULTRIX V4.2. You'll get (in a separate directory tree) unmolested
X11R4 header files and libraries. That'll help with xdvi and GNUplot, most
likely.
