In article <1991Aug8.213559.23534@batcomputer.tn.cornell.edu>, rogerj@theory.TC.Cornell.EDU (Roger Jagoda) writes:
> We run Xinu and DEQNAs are no problem. BTW, what is so special
> about the DELQA that makes DECnet work where the DEQNA won't?
> Seems like a scam from DEC to get more $$$s out of the VMS-hooked
> croud.

  Well, there are a number of things, which may or may not matter to you.

  o The DEQNA is not 802.3 compliant - it's Ethernet II only
  o The DEQNA can't handle back-to-back packets well
  o Under heavy loads or unusual situations, the DEQNA will corrupt data

  So if you are running on a lightly-loaded Ethernet II network with (for ex-
ample) no Sun NFS traffic, using protocols which include additional checksums,
you should be fine.

  Most VMS systems with DEQNA's are running Local Area VAXcluster, which can
produce substantial traffic (in back-to-back packets, even).

  I have 7 DELQA's here, only 2 of which are running in VAX/VMS systems. The
others are in PDP-11's. I swapped them all out because of the greater relia-
bility of the DELQA.

  There were _lots_ of revisions to the DEQNA. The current rev is something
like N6. By that time, all the bugs that were fixable were fixed, or the VMS
drivers were modified to work around the remaining ones. The DEQNA just doesn't
have enough horsepower to do anything more. It has an 803x microcontroller,
the same chip used in the IBM PC's keyboard. The DELQA uses a 68000.

