In article <1991Aug8.211841.23623@oakhill.sps.mot.com>, guri@oakhill.sps.mot.com (Gurvinder Singh Ahluwalia) writes:
> 
> ---------
> We have an AGS+ cisco (1-ENVM, 1-CSC3, 4-2E2T, 1-cBus) at PROM level 8.2(3) 
> running as a DECnet Level-2 router for the Austin site, besides wearing
> its IP hat as well.  No other node in the site (DECnet area) provides 
> Level-2 routing.

... 
> 
> Any ideas what it takes to fix these routing update losses?
> 

We had the same problem. The trick is to use *another* router than the 
cisco or the fp, and let original dec equipment doing the job. According 
to some hacks and additional advice from our national network staff (thanks, 
T.B.), our routing now looks as follows: 

UNIBA>show dec route

  Node       Cost  Hops   Next Hop to Node       *47.41 Expires  Prio

*(Area)        3     1   Ethernet0 -> 47.41		
						-> This is another cisco box.

*47.2          3     1   Ethernet0 -> 47.2          42     64    V
						-> a area routing VAX. 

*47.3          0     0     (Local) -> 47.3
						-> The cisco where I'm on, 
						   with prio 45. 

*47.16         3     1   Ethernet1 -> 47.16         41     64    V
						-> another area routing vax, 
						   on another segment. 

*47.40         3     1   Ethernet0 -> 47.40         38     64    V
						-> another  area routing VAX. 

*47.41         3     1   Ethernet0 -> 47.41         74     50    V
						-> another cisco box. 

*47.42         3     1   Ethernet0 -> 47.42         39     64    V
						-> a DEC router 
 
*47.53         3     1   Ethernet0 -> 47.53         65      1    V
						-> a FastPath. 

*47.58         4     1     Serial2 -> 47.58        210     44    V
						-> another cisco box 
						   beyond a modem. 

*47.59         4     1     Serial4 -> 47.59        188     44    V
						-> another cisco box
						   beyond a modem.  

*47.1022       3     1   Ethernet1 -> 47.1022       61      1    V
						-> another  FastPath. 


Rules say that the router with the highest priority number does 
the job, and if there are several with the same high priority, use the one 
with the highest node number. (here: 47.42). In our case, we use a 
DEC router which is also serving other lines with decnet only.
 
cisco apparently does fight against shiva. This way, another router takes the 
lead and the problem is solved. Neither of the two vendors or support staff 
was able to explain to me *why* it works that way. 


Regards 
Reinhard 


