In comp.sys.dec "Mark" <TQ-6MU=O@linac.fnal.gov> writes:

>In article <541@grce.UUCP>, joe@grc.genroco.com writes:
>> 
>> Does anyone know of some Ultrix/UNIX software which emulates a 
>> PDP-11/LSI-11 environment?  It could be an OS emulator, much
>> in the way that SoftPC is a DOS emulator under Ultrix.  Or it
>> could be a complete system emulator under which you can run
>> a PDP-11 OS, much like the old PDP-10 emulator which ran on
>> PDP-11s.
>> 
>> Does anything even remotely like this exist?

>Why would you want this?  Used PDP-11s are dirt cheap.  Such an "emulator"
>would probably have abysmal performance, anyway.

To take the second point first: there are plenty of folks around with 
large quantities of PDP-11 software performing useful tasks.  The cost
of porting these to VMS or Unix can be considerable - not all PDP-11
operating systems were RSX!  At the same time, the cost of maintaining
high-availability PDP-11 systems is sky-high - not everyone using PDP-11's
is a hobbyist.  (We provide information services related to earthquakes
and *must* be in a position to respond in minutes - we've almost completed
our migration to VMS & Unix).  In some circumstances, it could be easier to
take this particular mountain to the prophet.

To answer the original point: I've been taking an interest in this for
some time.  I found a piece of software by Ozalp Babaoglu and others for
ftp (a cornell machine, I think - archie can no doubt tell you which),
which emulates a machine with pretty well the entire '11 instruction set.
The docs suggest that the speed of the emulator running on a modern
workstation is comparable with that of a mid-level '11 (a /23, say) -
which is not out of line with the performance of SoftPC.

Unfortunately, it runs its own OS, with its own file system.  The memory
management system also differs from the '11.  Nevertheless, I suspect that
the amount of work needed to persuade it to boot a simple '11 OS such as
CTS or RT-11SJ is not huge.  I've had a copy sitting on my workstation
for a few months, but I've not found time to look at the possibility
seriously.  The name of the package is 'CHIP'.  I haven't even dared to
ask our DEC rep. about licensing RT-11 for it :-)  

Russ Evans
BGS Edinburgh                                   e_gs18@va.nmh.ac.uk


