Remember the recent comments about Ultrix `man' command brain damage?
As part of configuring our new 5500 servers with Ultrix 4.2, we
pitched DEC man and installed Tom Christiansens perl-based man.
Not only does it run fine and do "the right thing", its `makewhatis'
found a number of minor errors in the DEC man pages.  I've included
them below:

swap_words.3 .so references non-existent man3/swap_lw_bytes.3
swap_word_bytes.3 .so references non-existent man3/swap_lw_bytes.3
XtRemoveEventHandler was a .so alias for XtAddEventHandler.3Xt, but XtAddEventHandler.3Xt's NAME section doesn't know it!
XtRemoveRawEventHandler was a .so alias for XtAddEventHandler.3Xt, but XtAddEventHandler.3Xt's NAME section doesn't know it!
XModifierKeymap was a .so alias for XChangeKeyboardMapping.3X11, but XChangeKeyboardMapping.3X11's NAME section doesn't know it!
XFreeModifierMap was a .so alias for XChangeKeyboardMapping.3X11, but XChangeKeyboardMapping.3X11's NAME section doesn't know it!
local was a .so alias for rc.8, but rc.8's NAME section doesn't know it!
mh-tailor was a .so alias for mtstailor.5mh, but mtstailor.5mh's NAME section doesn't know it!

By the by, it created the whatis database in a mere 1 minute, 42 seconds.
Gee, maybe DEC should be running this . . . . :-)

Tom's man command suite is available for anon ftp from convex.com, in the
directory pub/perl/programs/man.shar.Z.  We're using it with perl 4.0.10.
