In article <7034@fuhainf.fernuni-hagen.de>, rosenb@fuhainf2.fernuni-hagen.de (Hans-Juergen Rosenberger) writes...
>does anybody know, why a vt220 eats all NUL-Bytes?
>...Is this right? Does it mean, that one will never be
>able to use a ibm-prorinter as a bypass-printer on a vt220?

This is expected behavior. One consequence of this behavior is that an IBM
Proprinter (or emulator) is effectively useless. (To mention the obvious: note
that this is not a supported configuration.)

What you are experiencing is a clash of two encoding standards: ISO 6429, the
standard to which communication with a VT220 conforms, allows NULL (0/0) to be
inserted and removed with no loss of information in the data stream--they are
not significant; in the de facto encoding used to communicate with an IBM
Proprinter, the 0/0 character is significant in command encoding.

Tim Lasko, Digital Equipment Corp., Westford MA  (lasko@regent.enet.dec.com)
Disclaimer: My opinions are my own; the facts can speak for themselves.


