---------------------- GENERAL DESCRIPTION ----------------------

>I've got two VAXstation 2000's I'd like to retire.  I'm interested in
>using VAX EWS, a download-at-boot-time VT1200 emulation.  In short, when
>the VS2000 powers up, it downloads the emulation from some server
>machine and then runs it.  This package basically turns the VS2000 into
>a VT1200 X terminal.
> 

EWS turns a VAXstation 2000 into a VT1300 X terminal, which is a bit
different than a VT1200.  The VT1200 supports LAT and TCP/IP as transports; 
the 1300 supports DECnet and TCP/IP.  Also, the VT1200 does LAT terminal 
emulation locally, and has more local "smarts" than the 1300, which loads 
its brains over the network only and starts sessions only with a specific 
host (generally the load host, although you can change that).

Actually, EWS is the *same* software that the VT1300 uses!

-------------------- HARDWARE REQUIRED -------------------------

>I called the Digital Tech Assistance number, but they could only say
>that the package requires a "standard" VS2000.  They weren't sure if
>the emulation required a certain amount of memory or a local disk.  Any
>netters out there with the answers?
>

You want a MINIMUM of 6MB of memory.  A color board will certainly speed
things up too, given the slow power (.9 VUPS) of the VS2000 cpu.

You don't need a local disk.  *BUT* in my experience unless you have a 
load panel on the power supply in the VS2000 you don't want to pull the
disk drives because sometimes you aren't loading enough 12V.  A sympton of
this is that the system won't come up to self-test *but* it will if you
disconnect the ethernet board.

You have to tell the VS2000 to boot of the ethernet.  One of the self 
tests let you set the boot device.

There are some problems with VS2000 with ROM v2.1, not booting on the 
first try.  If your VS2000 are still under maintenance, get DEC to 
upgrade the ROMS to v2.2, otherwise you'll just have to try booting until 
the host responds (2-3 tries).  The DECNET error you'll get is something 
like 'Protocol line error'. 

---------------------- PERFORMANCE ---------------------------

>If you've used this package, have you noticed any changes in the X
>performance?  When we ran Ultrix 3.x on the VS2000's, they weren't
>known for their windowing speed (even with 14 Mb of RAM).
> 

Performance depends heavily on the cpu running the X application.  If you're
running a MicroVAX II displaying to a VS2000 running EWS then it should
perform about 2x better.

A 2000 running EWS, talking to a VAX 6320 running VMS, performs adequately.

I am currently running 5 VS2000's (6 MB, 2 with color 3 without) off of a 
VAX 4000-300 running VMS and we don't notice the load when they are idle
and the load is reasonable when they are all in use.  (We do other things
on the 4000 as well...the VS2000's are spare/backup/extra terminals for my
group.

Very much improved performance over booted as a satellite in a VMS cluster.

I'd suggest that you should have aproximately .5 - 1.5 vups per EWS seat.

It's given the VS2000s we have a second lease on life and saved
them from the salvage heap.

--------------------- SOME CAVEATS -----------------------------

EWS loads off of VMS or Ultrix.  You need MOP installed on your Ultrix box in
order to load EWS.  I believe MOP is part of the standard UWS/Ultrix release.

If you want to use the thing to access a Unix host 
you have to create at least one terminal session telnet'ing from the VAX.

If you use a package that requires extra fonts, such as DECWRITE, make sure 
you include these fonts in the 'NODE.DAT' file in the EWS directory. DO 
NOT FORGET THE NODE NUMBER (NOT NAME) in the font directory specification...
read the manual.

