I posted a request for info about VAXELN to the net a few days
ago. Al Cook was kind enough to reply by email. I asked more involved
questions again by email and Mr. Cook graciously replied.  I thought
the replies were helpful enough to post - here it is (the lines preceeded
with "<" are my questions - normal lines are Mr. Cook's responses
++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++

Hello Jerry,

 Your looking for a lot of general information.  It may be better to try the
internet realtime notes file so everyone would benefit from a response.  I'll
take a pass at answering your questions.  There is also a very good one week
class that is done in Atlanta that would cover a lot of information.  If
your looking for more expertise at your office, I do an on-site class that
has been very well accepted.
 
>   I don't know anything about it except that it is a real time
>operating system for Vaxes - I know something about VMS and I was
>asked about a project that  is doing using a Vax. I assumed they
>were talking VMS andI answered accordingly - (blah blah QIO blah blah)
It was then pointed out to me that I was dealing with VAXELN - I am
>still accepted as a knowledgeable person although I know nothing about
>VAXELN and those people were so pleased with my answer :o  that they
>will use me again - ulp!!
>

Sounds familiar.
 
>  For now I just need some general information :
> 
>  Exactly what is VAXELN? What does VAXELN mean? 

VAXELN is an embedded, realtime operating system for a VAX target system.  It is
completely memory resident (runs diskless and there is no page-swapping).  It is
extended with MANY realtime related system service calls and utilities.  Memory
sharing, process synchronization, message passing, for example, can be
accomplished without in-depth system knowledge.  Drivers can be written in a
higher order language.  It includes full support for DECnet, TCP/IP and 
DECwindows.

Development is done on VMS to produce a system image that is down-line loaded
(or booted) onto a target VAX.  All VAXes are not supported but all of the ones
that have a realtime model are.  This includes all Q-bus VAXes, the KA800
co-processor, the KAV30 (VAX on a VME) and all 6000s.  Debugging is done
remotely with a fully symbolic debugger.

This is NOT a stripped down VMS, but an operating system developed from scratch
to be used in a realtime environment.


>Is it related to something else? 

No, it's a bit like MicroPower Pascal in concept but was developed separately.
Or maybe I should say, "Define related."

>Why is it not "pushed"? 

Why doesn't Digital advertise more?  Got me.  It is definitely a 'real' product.
I have been involved with it in one capacity or another (telephone support,
engineering and now field sales support) since 1984.  It makes a profit and is
used around the world.  It may not be pushed, but it is somewhat successful 
anyway.  It's also used extensively internally.  The LPS40/20, the VT1300 and
the VAX 9000 console are all software based on VAXELN.  I also believe MITRE
is already a customer.

>What does it do that VMS doesn't?  

Nothing.  We are still talking about a VAX, the architecture is still the same.
What is doesn't do is require an expert to 'tune' it for realtime.  It is 
intended to be used in realtime and has those extensions that a realtime system
would require.  Multi-processing (threads) within a single virtual space,
semaphores, mutexes, shared memory and easily written drivers.  Development time
should be quite a bit shorter for a realtime application with VAXELN.

>Is there an ADA compiler for it? 

Yes, that's my personal area of expertise.  Supported languages are EPascal
(a compiler specifically written for VAXELN), FORTRAN, C and Ada.  Languages
can be mixed at will.  Mixed development at the procedure, process (task) and
job (program) level is supported.

>How about development environments? 

Any VAX/VMS system.  It is fully CASE supported and there is a symbolic
remote debugger.

>Where can I get some documentation? 

Hmmmm, check your local office.  Ask for a copy of :

Introduction to VAXELN				AA-JL11D-TE
VAX Ada and VAXELN Ada Technical Summary	AA-GS98B-TE
Realtime User's Guide				AA-PE1WA-TE
Realtime Products Technical Summary		AA-PBSVA-TE
 

 
 
>                                    Thanks for all present and future help

My pleasure.

