In article <1991Aug6.171544.27787@bronze.ucs.indiana.edu>, lfischer@goliath (Leonard Fischer) writes:
   > I have a couple of questions about DecNet.
   > 
   > First, what is it?

  DECnet is DEC's proprietary networking software. It allows users to link VAXes, PDP's
  etc. Provides pretty well all the standard networking facilities, file transfer,
  remote login  etc. There are also implementations for smaller machines like IBM PC's
  (DECnet/DOS, from DEC), Amigas (two versions, from Syndesis & Interworks I think).
  Several Unix vendors like SUN also have implementations.

   > Second, does it have the ability to access the internet?

  DECnet uses its own protocols, which are not the same as those used by the internet,
  however there are various types of gateway software which can provide a link between
  a DECnet network and the internet. Some gateways are VAXes running both kinds
  of networking software, others are standalone units. 

   > 
   > I have a friend in Italy who says the only access he has to global  
   > communications is via his company's DecNet. However, he doesn't know if DecNet  
   > places him on the internet.

  If his company has a gateway to the internet then he should be able to use that. 
  He would really need to talk to someone in charge of his corporate network. The
  company I used to work for had such a gateway for years and I only found out
  about it by chance. :-). If he ever sees mail messages with source addresses of
  the form   ABCD::"fred@here.there.everywhere"  then there is a fair chance that
  node ABCD is a gateway.

   > 
   > Any suggestions I might be able to give him?
   > 
   > Thanks. I'm not real familiar with Dec stuff. I do Macs and PCs.
   > 
   > 
   > Leonard Fischer
   > Indiana University-Purdue University at Indianapolis
